// Written by shared/tools/build_refs.py from notes/refs/*.json. Do not edit by hand.
const BOOKS = {"AIE": {"title": "AI Engineering", "authors": "Chip Huyen (O'Reilly)"}, "HOLLM": {"title": "Hands-On Large Language Models", "authors": "Jay Alammar and Maarten Grootendorst (O'Reilly)"}, "PE": {"title": "Prompt Engineering for LLMs", "authors": "John Berryman and Albert Ziegler (O'Reilly)"}, "DDIA": {"title": "Designing Data-Intensive Applications", "authors": "Martin Kleppmann (O'Reilly)"}, "DMLS": {"title": "Designing Machine Learning Systems", "authors": "Chip Huyen (O'Reilly)"}};
const BOOK_REFS = {"AIE-1": {"chapter": "Introduction to Building AI Applications with Foundation Models", "about": "This chapter explains why AI engineering grew so fast: big models are now sold as a service, so anyone can build on them. It then covers common use cases, how to plan an AI product, and how the AI engineering stack differs from classic ML engineering.", "ideas": ["A demo is cheap and a product is not: the book says the last stretch of quality (for example 80% to 95%) can take several times longer than the first.", "Decide the role of AI in your product early: critical or optional, reactive or proactive, and how much a human stays in the loop. These choices set the quality and speed you need.", "Define success in business terms first (share of requests automated, time saved), then set a usefulness threshold for quality, latency and cost per request.", "AI engineering is mostly about adapting and evaluating someone else's model, not training your own. Open-ended outputs make evaluation the hard part.", "Start with prompt-based adaptation, which needs little data. Move to finetuning only when prompts are not enough.", "Plan for maintenance: models, prices and rules change quickly, so you need versioning and evaluation in place before you swap a model."], "read_when": "Read it when you are deciding whether and how to build an AI feature, or when you need to explain to your team how this work differs from ordinary ML or back-end work.", "sections": {"Three Layers of the AI Stack": "Splits any AI application into three layers: application development (prompts, context, evaluation, interface), model development (training, finetuning, datasets, inference optimization) and infrastructure (serving, data and compute, monitoring). A builder usually starts at the top layer and goes down only when needed.", "AI Engineering Versus ML Engineering": "Names three differences: you use a model someone else trained, so the work shifts to adapting it; models are bigger and slower, so efficiency matters more; and outputs are open-ended, which makes evaluation much harder. It also separates prompt-based adaptation, which leaves weights alone, from finetuning, which changes them.", "Planning AI Applications": "Says a cool demo is easy and a profitable product is hard, so think before building. Start with why you build (a threat, a chance to gain, or fear of being late), whether to build or buy, and what moat protects you. It names technology, data and distribution as the three kinds of advantage.", "The role of AI and humans in the application": "Describes three design choices: AI is critical or complementary, features are reactive or proactive, and they update continually or periodically. It also introduces human-in-the-loop and the Crawl-Walk-Run path, where automation grows as the acceptance rate of AI suggestions proves itself.", "Setting Expectations": "Tells you to define success as business impact first, such as the share of messages automated or labour saved. Then set a usefulness threshold across quality, latency (time to first token, time per output token, total), and cost per request, so a weak product is not shown to customers.", "Milestone Planning": "Says to evaluate existing models first, because the stronger they are, the less you must build. Warns about the last-mile problem: a demo takes a weekend, but going from good to great takes months, and LinkedIn needed one month for 80% and four more for 95%. It also covers the upkeep burden from fast model, price and regulation changes.", "Conversational Bots": "Lists chatbots as a versatile use case: customer support, product copilots that guide people through tasks, companions, voice assistants and smart game characters. For companies, support bots are the most popular because they answer faster and cut cost.", "Information Aggregation": "Covers using AI to summarize and distill emails, documents and meetings, including the talk-to-your-docs pattern. One example is Instacart's internal prompt template that turns messy conversations into facts, open questions and action items.", "Data Organization": "Explains that photos, videos, contracts and logs are unstructured, and AI can make them searchable by describing them or extracting structured fields. Examples run from reading receipts and licenses to pulling data out of contracts and reports.", "Workflow Automation": "Presents automation of repetitive tasks such as invoicing, lead management and data entry as the end goal. Many tasks need outside tools, and AI that can plan and use tools is called an agent."}}, "AIE-2": {"chapter": "Understanding Foundation Models", "about": "This chapter explains the design choices that make foundation models differ: training data, architecture and size, and post-training. It spends its second half on sampling, which is how a model turns probabilities into text, and on why that makes outputs inconsistent and sometimes false.", "ideas": ["A model picks each next token by sampling from a probability distribution, so the same prompt can give different answers.", "Temperature reshapes those probabilities. Low values give consistent but duller output, high values give varied but less coherent output, and 0 in practice means always pick the top token.", "Generating several answers and choosing one (by highest average logprob, a verifier, a heuristic or majority vote) improves quality, but each extra answer costs about the same as the first.", "To get structured output, you can prompt, post-process, retry, constrain sampling or finetune. The first three are light fixes, the last two are stronger.", "Fixing temperature, top-p, top-k and seed reduces inconsistency but never guarantees it, because hardware can also change the result.", "Hallucination has two proposed causes: the model treats its own earlier output as fact, and training teaches it to imitate answers that rely on knowledge it lacks."], "read_when": "Read the sampling part when your app gives different answers to the same input, returns broken JSON, or you need to decide how to set temperature and handle hallucination.", "sections": {"Sampling Fundamentals": "Explains that a language model first computes a probability for every token in its vocabulary, turning raw scores (logits) into probabilities with softmax. Always taking the top token (greedy sampling) works for classification but makes text dull, so models instead sample in proportion to the probabilities.", "Sampling Strategies": "Covers the knobs that control sampling: temperature rescales scores to favour or flatten likely tokens, top-k and top-p limit which tokens are considered, and stopping conditions cap length. It also covers generating many answers and picking the best, and notes that logprobs are useful for debugging and classification but many providers expose them poorly.", "Structured Outputs": "Says production apps often need output in a fixed format, either because the task is semantic parsing such as text-to-SQL or because another program consumes it. It ranks five ways to get it: prompting, post-processing, retrying, constrained sampling and finetuning. Note that a provider's JSON mode only promises valid JSON, not correct content, and cut-off output can still break parsing.", "The Probabilistic Nature of AI": "States that because models sample, they are inconsistent (same input gives different outputs, or tiny input changes give very different ones) and they hallucinate. It suggests caching, fixed sampling settings and a fixed seed to reduce inconsistency, warns these never fully guarantee it, and presents two hypotheses for why hallucinations happen."}}, "AIE-3": {"chapter": "Evaluation Methodology", "about": "This chapter explains why evaluating open-ended AI output is hard and surveys the methods: exact checks (functional correctness, reference matching, similarity), AI as a judge, and comparative ranking. Its angle is to show how each method works and where it fails.", "ideas": ["Functional correctness, meaning whether the output does its job (code passes tests, a booking succeeds), is the best metric when you can automate it.", "Exact match only suits short, fixed answers. For longer text, lexical or semantic similarity to a reference works better, but needs good reference data.", "An AI judge is fast, cheap and works without reference answers, but it is a model plus a prompt, so changing either one gives you a different judge.", "Judge prompts should state the task, the criteria and the scoring system. Classification or a small 1 to 5 scale works better than wide numeric ranges, and examples help.", "AI judges are inconsistent and biased: they favour their own answers, the first option, and longer answers. Do not trust a judge whose model and prompt you cannot see.", "Add the cost of judging into your budget, and back it up with exact checks or human review."], "read_when": "Read it when you need to decide how to score your app's answers automatically, or when you want to know whether an LLM judge can be trusted.", "sections": {"Exact Evaluation": "Separates evaluation that gives an unambiguous result from subjective grading. It covers functional correctness (run the output and test it, as code benchmarks do with unit tests and the pass@k score) and comparison against reference data by exact match, lexical similarity (word and n-gram overlap, edit distance) and semantic similarity. It warns that exact match breaks on anything but short answers and that a good answer can score low if the references are poor.", "AI as a Judge": "Describes using a model to grade another model's output, now one of the most common production methods because it is fast, cheap and needs no reference answers. It gives prompt advice (state the task, criteria and scoring system, prefer classification or small scales, add examples) and lists the weaknesses: inconsistency, unstandardized criteria, extra cost and latency, and biases such as self-preference, first-position and verbosity bias. It concludes judges should be paired with exact checks or human review."}}, "AIE-4": {"chapter": "Evaluate AI Systems", "about": "This chapter shows how to use evaluation methods for your own application: choose criteria, pick a model, and build an evaluation pipeline. It also compares commercial APIs with self-hosted models and explains why public benchmarks cannot be fully trusted.", "ideas": ["Define evaluation criteria before you build (evaluation-driven development). Group them into domain capability, generation quality, instruction following, and cost and latency.", "Pick a model in four steps: drop models whose hard attributes do not fit, shortlist using public benchmarks, test with your own pipeline, then keep monitoring in production.", "Weigh API versus self-hosting on seven axes: data privacy, data lineage, performance, functionality, cost, control, and on-device use.", "Public benchmarks and leaderboards are only a shortlist tool, because they cover few capabilities and are often contaminated by test data in training sets.", "Evaluate each component of the system as well as the end-to-end result, and write a scoring guideline with examples for good and bad answers.", "Keep several evaluation sets (typical traffic, known failures, typos, out of scope inputs), and check that your set is large enough to give stable results."], "read_when": "Read it when you must choose between models or providers, or when you are designing the test set and scoring for your own AI feature.", "sections": {"Evaluation Criteria": "Argues that you should decide how you will evaluate an app before building it, and that evaluation is the biggest bottleneck to AI adoption. It sorts criteria into domain capability, generation capability (such as factual consistency and safety), instruction following, and cost and latency. It also covers how to measure each, for example checking factual consistency against given context with an AI judge.", "Model Selection Workflow": "Splits model attributes into hard ones you cannot change (license, size, your privacy policy) and soft ones you can improve. Then it gives a four-step loop: filter by hard attributes, shortlist with public data, test with your own pipeline, and monitor in production. It notes the steps repeat as you learn more.", "Model Build Versus Buy": "Compares commercial APIs with hosting an open model along seven axes: data privacy, data lineage, performance, functionality, cost, control, and on-device use. APIs are easier and usually strongest but cost per use and can change under you; self-hosting gives control and logprobs but needs engineering effort. It advises choosing a standard API shape so models are easy to swap.", "Navigate Public Benchmarks": "Explains that benchmarks and leaderboards help shortlist models but have limits: leaderboards use few benchmarks, scores need weighting because scales differ, and data contamination (training on the test set) inflates results. Treat public scores as a way to pick a few models, then test them on your own data.", "Design Your Evaluation Pipeline": "Gives a three-step recipe: evaluate every component and each turn and task, write a clear evaluation guideline with a scoring rubric tied to business metrics, and choose methods and annotated data. It suggests mixing cheap checks on all data with costly judges on a sample, slicing data into several test sets, checking the sample size with bootstrapping, and logging every evaluation variable."}}, "AIE-5": {"chapter": "Prompt Engineering", "about": "This chapter covers how to write prompts that work and how to defend an application against prompt attacks. It treats prompt engineering as a real experimental practice that needs versioning and evaluation, not word fiddling.", "ideas": ["Write clear, explicit instructions, give examples, state the output format, and give enough context. Prompt tricks that depend on one model's quirks age fast.", "Split a complex task into smaller prompts chained together. This helps monitoring, debugging and parallel runs, and GoDaddy found it improved results and cut tokens.", "Ask the model to reason step by step or to critique its own answer, but expect higher latency and cost.", "Keep prompts out of code and version them, ideally in a catalog where each app can pin the version it uses.", "Models use the start and end of a long prompt better than the middle. Test long prompts with a needle-in-a-haystack check.", "Defend in layers (model, prompt, system), assume your system prompt will leak, and never let generated actions run unchecked. Risk can be reduced but not removed."], "read_when": "Read it when you are writing or reorganizing the prompts in your app, or when you need to threat-model prompt injection before giving a model tools or private data.", "sections": {"System Prompt and User Prompt": "Explains that the system prompt carries the developer's task description and the user prompt carries the task, but a model merges them into one prompt using its own chat template. A wrong template fails silently, so the advice is to follow the template exactly and print the final prompt before sending. A system prompt may help because it comes first or because the model was trained to favour it.", "Context Length and Context Efficiency": "Notes that context limits have grown from about 1K tokens to millions, but models do not use all positions equally well: they handle the start and end of a prompt better than the middle. It recommends a needle-in-a-haystack test, with private data, to see how your model copes, and shortening prompts if quality drops as context grows.", "Prompt Engineering Best Practices": "Collects techniques that work across models: write clear instructions, give a persona and examples, state the output format and end markers, supply enough context, split complex tasks into subtasks, and let the model think step by step. It also says to iterate, to version and test prompts against fixed data, and to evaluate each prompt within the whole system.", "Organize and Version Prompts": "Recommends keeping prompts separate from code, which makes them reusable, testable, readable and open to subject-matter experts. It adds metadata such as model and sampling settings. Versioning prompts in git forces every app that shares a prompt onto the new version, so larger teams use a prompt catalog where each app picks a version.", "Defensive Prompt Engineering": "Introduces the three kinds of prompt attack (prompt extraction, jailbreaking and injection, and information extraction) and the harm they can cause, from unauthorized tool or code execution and data leaks to brand damage. It frames the rest of the chapter as how each attack works and how to defend.", "Proprietary Prompts and Reverse Prompt Engineering": "Says teams guard their prompts, and attackers try to recover them by analysing outputs or tricking the model into repeating its instructions. Many supposedly leaked prompts are just model hallucinations. The advice is to write your system prompt as if it will become public, and it argues that prompts are a maintenance liability more than a moat.", "Jailbreaking and Prompt Injection": "Defines jailbreaking as getting a model past its safety rules and prompt injection as smuggling instructions into user input, and says attacks work because models follow instructions. It describes manual tricks (obfuscation, format changes, roleplay), automated attacks, and indirect injection, where malicious text sits in a web page, email or database record that the model reads through a tool.", "Information Extraction": "Describes attacks that pull out a model's training data or the private data in its context, for data theft, privacy violations or copyright copying. Models can memorize and regurgitate text, larger models more so, and a repeated-word prompt once made ChatGPT leak training text. Filters on personal data in requests and responses reduce the risk.", "Defenses Against Prompt Attacks": "Recommends learning your weak points with red teaming and attack-test tools, and measuring both the violation rate and the false refusal rate. Defenses sit at three levels: model (an instruction hierarchy where tool output ranks lowest), prompt (explicit prohibitions, repeating the instruction after the input), and system (isolating code execution, human approval for impactful actions, input and output guardrails). It warns that risk can never be fully removed."}}, "AIE-6": {"chapter": "RAG and Agents", "about": "This chapter covers the two main ways to give a model the right context for each query: retrieval-augmented generation, and agents that use tools. It also covers memory, which helps both handle more information than fits in the context window.", "ideas": ["A RAG system has a retriever and a generator, and its quality depends mostly on the retriever. Evaluate retrieval (context precision and recall), the final answers and the embeddings separately.", "Term-based search (BM25) is fast, cheap and strong out of the box but misses meaning. Embedding search finds meaning but can blur exact terms such as error codes. Production systems often combine them.", "Retrieval can be improved with sensible chunking (size, overlap), reranking, rewriting the query so it stands alone, and adding context or metadata to each chunk.", "An agent is a model plus an environment and a set of tools, with read-only and write actions. Write actions need extra care and often human approval.", "Agent errors compound: 95% accuracy per step falls to about 60% over 10 steps. Plan first, validate the plan, then execute, and reflect after steps.", "Memory has three layers (internal knowledge, short-term context, long-term external storage), and a simple first-in-first-out policy can drop the most important early messages."], "read_when": "Read it when you are building a retrieval pipeline, choosing between keyword and vector search, or giving a model tools and need to know how agents plan and fail.", "sections": {"RAG": "Defines RAG as improving a model's answer by retrieving relevant information from external sources such as a database, past chats or the web. It argues that long context windows will not replace RAG: data keeps growing, models use long context poorly, and every extra token costs money and time. It treats context construction as the counterpart of feature engineering in classic ML.", "RAG Architecture": "Describes a RAG system as a retriever (which indexes data and then queries it) plus a generator that answers from what was retrieved. Documents are split into chunks, the best chunks are joined with the user prompt, and the result goes to the model. The retriever's quality largely decides whether the system works.", "Embedding-based retrieval": "Explains that embedding search ranks documents by meaning, not shared words: chunks are turned into vectors at index time, the query is embedded with the same model, and the k closest vectors are returned. Exact k-nearest-neighbour search is precise but slow, so large datasets use approximate methods such as LSH, HNSW, product quantization, IVF and Annoy.", "Term-based retrieval": "Describes keyword retrieval, which ranks documents by how often a query term appears (term frequency) weighted by how rare the term is across documents (inverse document frequency). BM25 adjusts this for document length, runs on an inverted index in tools like Elasticsearch, and remains a strong baseline that is hard to beat.", "Comparing retrieval algorithms": "Weighs the two approaches: term-based is faster, cheaper and good out of the box but hard to improve, while embedding-based can win after tuning but is costlier and can blur exact terms like error codes. It recommends measuring context precision and recall, ranking metrics and embedding quality, and notes that vector storage can cost a large share of model spending.", "Combining retrieval algorithms": "Says production systems usually mix methods, called hybrid search. They can run in sequence (a cheap retriever fetches candidates, a more precise step reranks them) or in parallel, merging the ranked lists with reciprocal rank fusion, which sums a score of one over the rank (plus a constant) from each retriever.", "Retrieval Optimization": "Introduces four tactics for getting the right documents back: chunking strategy, reranking, query rewriting and contextual retrieval. It also gives a checklist for evaluating a retrieval product, such as hybrid support, scalability, indexing time, query latency and pricing.", "Reranking": "Says a second pass can reorder the retriever's first results to be more accurate, especially when you must cut how many documents go into the prompt. Results can also be reranked by recency for time-sensitive uses. In context reranking, exact position matters less than in search, though start and end positions still help.", "Query rewriting": "Shows that a follow-up such as a bare name is ambiguous, so it should be rewritten into a standalone question before retrieval, either with heuristics or by prompting a model. If rewriting needs facts the system lacks, such as who someone's wife is, the model should say it cannot resolve the query instead of inventing an answer.", "Contextual retrieval": "Recommends attaching context to each chunk so it is easier to find: keywords, tags, extracted entities such as an error code, questions the chunk answers, or a short model-written note placing the chunk within its source document (Anthropic's method). The augmented chunk is what gets indexed.", "Chunking strategy": "Covers how to split documents: by fixed size in characters, words, sentences or paragraphs, or recursively by smaller units, with a small overlap so boundary information survives. Smaller chunks add variety but can lose context and mean more vectors to store and search. There is no universal best size, so test.", "RAG Beyond Texts": "Extends RAG to other data. Multimodal RAG retrieves images or other media with captions or a shared multimodal embedding model such as CLIP. Tabular data needs a different flow: a model writes a SQL query from the question and table schemas, the query runs, and the result is used to write the answer.", "RAG with tabular data": "Shows the flow for questions that need table data: text-to-SQL, SQL execution, then answer generation from the result. If there are too many tables to fit in the prompt, add a step that picks which tables are relevant first.", "Agents": "Frames agents as the more general pattern: a model that can use tools to gather information and act, which makes RAG a special case. It warns that this section is more experimental than the rest of the book because there is no settled framework for building or evaluating agents.", "Agent Overview": "Defines an agent as something that perceives an environment and acts on it, characterized by that environment and its tools. It notes that agents need stronger models because errors compound across steps (95% per step is about 60% over 10 steps) and because failures matter more once tools can change things.", "Tools": "Groups tools into knowledge augmentation (retrievers, SQL, web browsing), capability extension (a calculator, a code interpreter, converters, captioning) and write actions that change the world. It stresses that write actions are risky and need trust and safeguards, and that more tools means more capability but harder use.", "Function calling": "Explains how tool use works in APIs: you declare tools with a name, parameters and description, choose which are allowed per query (required, none or auto), and the model replies with the tool name and arguments for you to execute. Some APIs ensure the function is valid but cannot ensure the argument values are right, so log and inspect them.", "Planning": "Describes planning as breaking a task into steps, and says to separate planning from execution so a plan is validated by simple rules or a model before it runs. It covers intent classification to choose tools, human involvement for risky steps, and doubts about whether language models plan well.", "Planning granularity": "Describes the trade-off between detailed plans (harder to write, easier to run) and high-level ones. It suggests hierarchical planning and writing plans in natural language rather than exact function names, so tool changes do not break the planner, with a cheaper translator turning steps into calls.", "Complex plans": "Lists control flows a plan can use (sequential, parallel, if, for loop) and notes that with agents the model decides them, which makes non-sequential plans harder. It also covers reflection and error correction, including the ReAct pattern of thought, action, observation and Reflexion, which are fairly easy to add and can help a lot but cost tokens and latency.", "Agent Failure Modes and Evaluation": "Says to evaluate an agent by listing its failure modes and measuring each. Planning failures include invalid tools, bad parameters or values, missing constraints and wrongly claiming success; tool failures mean wrong tool output; efficiency covers steps, cost and time. Print every tool call and output so you can inspect them.", "Memory": "Describes three kinds of memory: the model's internal knowledge, short-term memory (the context window) and long-term memory (external retrieval). Memory helps with overflow, keeping information across sessions, consistency and structured data. First-in-first-out trimming is simple but can lose the early messages that state the purpose, so summarising or reflecting on what to keep is often better."}}, "AIE-7": {"chapter": "Finetuning", "about": "This chapter explains when finetuning a model is worth the cost, how it compares with RAG, and why memory is its main bottleneck. It then covers memory-efficient methods, above all parameter-efficient finetuning and LoRA, plus model merging.", "ideas": ["Finetuning is usually tried only after serious, systematic prompting experiments. Many cases where prompting seems not to work turn out to come from sloppy prompt tests.", "Choose by failure type: if the model lacks or has outdated information, use RAG; if its behaviour is wrong (format, style, relevance), consider finetuning. The book sums this up as form versus facts.", "If you have both kinds of problems, start with RAG, and start with simple keyword search before vector databases.", "Finetuning for one task can make a model worse at others, and it brings ongoing costs: data, ML skill, serving, and a policy for when a newer base model replaces yours.", "Full finetuning of a 7B model needs about 56 GB of memory with a common optimizer, which is why parameter-efficient methods are popular.", "LoRA trains two small matrices that are merged back into the original weights, so it adds almost no inference latency and needs far fewer trainable parameters."], "read_when": "Read it when someone on your team proposes finetuning and you need to decide whether RAG or better prompts would do, or when you want to understand what LoRA is.", "sections": {"When to Finetune": "Says finetuning needs far more than prompting does (data, hardware and ML talent), so it is normally attempted after extensive prompt experiments. The two are not exclusive, and real systems often use both.", "Reasons to Finetune": "Lists the main reasons: improving quality on your specific task or data format, such as an uncommon SQL dialect, enforcing structured outputs like JSON or YAML, and reducing bias with curated data. Small finetuned models can beat much larger general ones on a narrow task, and distillation trains a small model on a large model's outputs.", "Reasons Not to Finetune": "Warns that finetuning for one task can hurt others, needs costly data and training skill, requires you to serve and maintain the model, and can be overtaken by new base models. It says to start with prompting, noting that complaints about prompting often come from minimal, unsystematic tests, and that general models can beat domain-specific ones such as BloombergGPT.", "Finetuning and RAG": "Separates information-based failures (missing or outdated facts), which RAG fixes, from behaviour-based failures (wrong format, style or relevance), which finetuning can fix. One study found RAG on a base model beat finetuned models on current-events questions. With both problems, start with RAG, using simple term-based retrieval first, and combine the two later if needed.", "Finetuning Techniques": "Introduces memory-efficient ways to adapt models, centred on parameter-efficient finetuning, and also model merging, an experimental way to combine several models. The reason is that memory cost limits who can finetune large models.", "Parameter-Efficient Finetuning": "Contrasts full finetuning, where every weight is trained and a 7B model needs about 56 GB of memory, with partial finetuning, which saves memory but needs many parameters to match quality. PEFT adds a small number of trainable parameters (for example adapters) and reaches near-full quality with a few percent of them, and with fewer examples. The first adapters add some inference latency.", "LoRA": "Explains LoRA as splitting a weight update into two small matrices of a chosen rank, training only those, and merging the result back into the original weights, so there is no extra inference latency. It reports comparable or better results than full finetuning on GPT-3 with a tiny fraction of the parameters, and suggests it works because pre-trained models have a low intrinsic dimension."}}, "AIE-8": {"chapter": "Dataset Engineering", "about": "This chapter argues that model quality depends on training data quality, and walks through curating data, synthesizing it with AI, and processing it. It focuses on data for finetuning, which matters most to application developers.", "ideas": ["Think of data in three dimensions: quality, coverage (diversity) and quantity. The amount you need depends on the finetuning method, the task's difficulty and how good the base model already is.", "Different training goals need different data shapes: instruction and response pairs, preference triples (winning and losing answer), or sequences for self-supervised training.", "AI-generated data can add quantity, fill coverage gaps and sometimes beat human quality, but it needs verification, and training on unfiltered model output can degrade a model.", "Model distillation trains a small student model on a larger teacher's outputs. It can give a much cheaper model for deployment, but some licenses forbid using outputs to train other models.", "Processing matters as much as collection: inspect the data, remove duplicates and fix the format before training."], "read_when": "Read it when you plan to finetune or distill a model and need to know how much data, of what kind, and how to generate and check it.", "sections": {"Model Distillation": "Describes distillation as training a small student model to imitate a large teacher, usually on data the teacher generated, to get a smaller and faster model that keeps most of the quality (DistilBERT, Alpaca and BuzzFeed's cheaper model are examples). It adds that licenses may forbid using a model's outputs to train others, that synthetic data is not always distillation, and that training on unverified self-generated data can hurt."}}, "AIE-9": {"chapter": "Inference Optimization", "about": "This chapter is about making model serving faster and cheaper. It defines the metrics to track, explains the hardware and model-level bottlenecks, and covers techniques at the model level (compression, faster decoding) and the service level (batching, prompt caching).", "ideas": ["Total latency is roughly time to first token plus time per output token times the number of output tokens. Users judge the first token and the pace separately.", "Look at latency percentiles (p50, p90, p95, p99) rather than the average, because a few slow requests skew the mean.", "Throughput and cost trade off against latency. Goodput, which counts only requests that meet your latency targets, shows what users really get.", "GPU utilization from standard tools can be misleading. Model FLOP/s utilization and bandwidth utilization show how efficiently the hardware is really used.", "Prompt caching reuses repeated prompt prefixes such as a long system prompt, and can cut cost and time to first token a lot (the book cites savings of up to 90% cost with one provider).", "Streaming responses and batch APIs suit different needs: streaming lowers the wait for the first token, batch APIs suit work that is not urgent."], "read_when": "Read it when your app feels slow or the model bill is too high, and you want to know which metrics to measure and what levers exist, even if you only use a hosted API.", "sections": {"Inference Performance Metrics": "Defines the metrics to measure. Latency splits into time to first token and time per output token, so total latency is the first plus the second times the output length, and it should be read as percentiles rather than an average. It also covers throughput (output tokens per second, which drives cost), goodput (requests that meet your latency targets), and utilization measures such as model FLOP/s and bandwidth utilization, warning that plain GPU utilization can mislead."}}, "AIE-10": {"chapter": "AI Engineering Architecture and User Feedback", "about": "This chapter assembles the book's techniques into one application architecture, starting from a bare model call and adding context, guardrails, routing, caching and agent patterns step by step. It then covers monitoring, orchestration and how to collect and use user feedback.", "ideas": ["Start with the simplest design (query in, model answer out) and add a component only when a problem calls for it: context, guardrails, router and gateway, caches, then agent patterns.", "Guard both sides. Input guardrails stop private data leaking to external APIs and block attacks; output guardrails catch bad formats, hallucinations and unsafe content, often with a simple retry or a handoff to a human.", "A router (an intent classifier) sends each query to the right model or workflow and can reject out-of-scope requests cheaply. A gateway gives one safe entry point to many models, with access control, cost limits and fallbacks.", "Exact caching is simple but must never cache answers that depend on one user. Semantic caching is riskier because a wrong similarity match returns a wrong answer.", "Design observability in from the start: log every configuration, prompt, tool call and output, link events into traces, and watch for drift in prompts, user behaviour and the provider's model.", "Conversation gives rich implicit feedback (stopping early, rephrasing, regenerating, edits). Treat it as user data and respect privacy."], "read_when": "Read it when you are drawing the whole architecture of an AI application, or deciding what to log, cache, guard and measure before going to production.", "sections": {"AI Engineering Architecture": "Presents a general architecture built up in steps from the simplest form, a query sent to a model. It adds context construction, guardrails, a router and gateway, caching, and agent patterns, with monitoring and orchestration around them. It says to add components in the order your own needs dictate.", "Step 1. Enhance Context": "Says the first expansion is usually giving the model per-query context through retrieval and tools (web, news, weather and other APIs), likening it to feature engineering. Providers support this to different degrees, for example in upload limits and parallel tool execution, so compare them with your needs.", "Enhance Context": "Says the first expansion is usually giving the model per-query context through retrieval and tools (web, news, weather and other APIs), likening it to feature engineering. Providers support this to different degrees, for example in upload limits and parallel tool execution, so compare them with your needs.", "Step 2. Put in Guardrails": "Introduces guardrails as protection placed wherever the system is exposed to risk, split into input and output guardrails. Risk can be reduced but never fully removed.", "Put in Guardrails": "Introduces guardrails as protection placed wherever the system is exposed to risk, split into input and output guardrails. Risk can be reduced but never fully removed.", "Input guardrails": "Explains that input guardrails guard against leaking private data to external APIs and against malicious prompts. Sensitive data detectors can block a query or mask items such as phone numbers with placeholders, and a reverse map restores them in the answer.", "Output guardrails": "Says output guardrails catch failures (empty or malformatted responses, hallucinations, toxic or private content) and define what to do next. Simple retries often work, optionally in parallel to save time at higher cost, and some cases are handed to humans, for example when sentiment turns angry. It also asks you to track false refusals.", "Guardrail implementation": "Notes the trade-offs: guardrails add latency, and output checks work badly with streaming because unsafe text may reach users before it is blocked. How many you need depends on whether you self-host or use an API, and ready-made options exist, though providers must balance safety against flexibility.", "Step 3. Add Model Router and Gateway": "Describes a router, usually an intent classifier, that sends queries to specialized or cheaper models, declines out-of-scope requests and asks for clarification on ambiguous ones. It also describes a gateway, a unified secure layer in front of all models that centralizes keys, access control, cost limits, fallbacks, logging and sometimes caching.", "Add Model Router and Gateway": "Describes a router, usually an intent classifier, that sends queries to specialized or cheaper models, declines out-of-scope requests and asks for clarification on ambiguous ones. It also describes a gateway, a unified secure layer in front of all models that centralizes keys, access control, cost limits, fallbacks, logging and sometimes caching.", "Step 4. Reduce Latency with Caches": "Covers system caching, as distinct from the inference caches in Chapter 9. Exact caching reuses results for identical requests and needs an eviction policy; it should skip user-specific or time-sensitive queries and can leak data if an answer containing one user's information is cached. Semantic caching matches similar queries by embedding, but depends on embeddings, a threshold and vector search, so its value is doubtful unless hit rates are high.", "Reduce Latency with Caches": "Covers system caching, as distinct from the inference caches in Chapter 9. Exact caching reuses results for identical requests and needs an eviction policy; it should skip user-specific or time-sensitive queries and can leak data if an answer containing one user's information is cached. Semantic caching matches similar queries by embedding, but depends on embeddings, a threshold and vector search, so its value is doubtful unless hit rates are high.", "Step 5. Add Agent Patterns": "Says the last step is letting the flow loop back, such as retrieving more after judging an answer incomplete, and allowing write actions like sending email or placing orders. Write actions add capability and risk, and a more complex system has more failure points and is harder to debug.", "Add Agent Patterns": "Says the last step is letting the flow loop back, such as retrieving more after judging an answer incomplete, and allowing write actions like sending email or placing orders. Write actions add capability and risk, and a more complex system has more failure points and is harder to debug.", "Monitoring and Observability": "Argues that observability should be designed in, not added later. It rates observability by time to detect, time to respond and change failure rate, and says to design metrics around the failures you fear: format errors, factual consistency, safety, length and cost, latency per user, and user signals. Metrics should be sliceable by user, release and prompt version.", "Logs and traces": "Explains that metrics show something went wrong while logs show what happened, so log everything: configuration, sampling settings, prompt template, user query, final prompt, outputs, tool calls and tool outputs, with IDs. Traces link these events into the full path of one request with timing and cost, so a failure can be pinned to a step. It adds drift detection for prompt, user behaviour and model changes.", "AI Pipeline Orchestration": "Describes an orchestrator as the tool that defines the components (models, data sources, tools) and chains them from query to answer, passing data between steps and flagging breaks. It recommends starting without one, since an orchestrator can hide details and add latency, and judging tools by integration, support for complex pipelines, and ease of use and scale.", "User Feedback": "Treats feedback as both an evaluation signal and proprietary training data, to be handled with privacy care. It separates explicit feedback (ratings, thumbs) from implicit feedback, and lists conversational signals such as early termination, corrections and rephrasing, complaints, regeneration, edits and conversation length. It also covers when and how to ask for feedback, and its limits."}}, "HOLLM-1": {"chapter": "An Introduction to Large Language Models", "about": "A scene-setting chapter that traces Language AI from bag-of-words through embeddings and attention to today's encoder-only and decoder-only models. It ends with the practical choices a builder faces: closed or open models, how much GPU memory you need, and loading a first model with Hugging Face Transformers.", "ideas": ["Training an LLM is usually two steps: pretraining on huge text to predict the next word, then fine-tuning for a task such as following instructions. Most teams only ever do the second step or none.", "A base model from pretraining does not follow instructions. The helpful chat behaviour comes from fine-tuning.", "Using an LLM means loading two things: the model and its tokenizer, which splits the text into tokens before the model sees it.", "Models can answer fluently and be wrong, and the chapter lists harmful output, intellectual property and regulation as risks to plan for.", "GPU memory (VRAM) decides which open models you can run at all, and quantization is the main way to fit a model in less of it.", "Frameworks change fast. The authors teach the shared ideas so you can pick up another framework easily."], "read_when": "Read it when you need a first map of the field, or when you are deciding between a hosted API and a model you run yourself.", "sections": {"Proprietary, Private Models": "Explains that closed models such as GPT-4 or Claude are reached through an API, so you need no GPU and no hosting skills, and they tend to perform best. The costs are a paid service, no way to fine-tune the model yourself, and your data going to the provider, which is a problem for something like patient records.", "Open Models": "Describes models whose weights are public, such as Llama, Mistral, Phi and Command R, that you can run locally, fine-tune and feed sensitive data. The price is needing strong hardware and setup knowledge, and licences vary, so some open models cannot be used commercially. The authors say they prefer open models where possible."}}, "HOLLM-2": {"chapter": "Tokens and Embeddings", "about": "Explains the two ideas under every LLM: tokens, the pieces text is cut into, and embeddings, the vectors that represent those pieces. It compares real tokenizers on the same text, then moves from token embeddings to whole-text embeddings and uses word embeddings for song recommendations.", "ideas": ["The model never sees your text. It sees a list of integer token IDs from the tokenizer, and it generates one token at a time.", "Most modern tokenizers use subword tokens, which handle new words and keep a smaller vocabulary than whole-word tokens.", "Tokenizers behave differently on capitalisation, numbers, code whitespace and other languages, depending on method, vocabulary size and training data.", "A pretrained model is tied to its own tokenizer and holds one embedding vector per token in its vocabulary.", "A text embedding model turns a sentence or document into one vector, which is the base for search, clustering and RAG.", "The same embedding idea works outside language, for example to recommend songs from playlists."], "read_when": "Read it when token counts, context limits or embedding vectors feel like black boxes and you want to see them in code.", "sections": {"LLM Tokenization": "Shows that a prompt is first turned into integer token IDs by a tokenizer, and the model's output IDs are decoded back to text by the same tokenizer. Using Phi-3 and Hugging Face Transformers, it prints the tokens of a prompt to show whole words, word pieces, punctuation and special start tokens. It then lists what decides the split: the tokenization method (BPE, WordPiece), the vocabulary and special-token choices, and the data the tokenizer was trained on.", "Token Embeddings": "Says that a language model stores one embedding vector for every token in its tokenizer's vocabulary, which is why a model cannot swap tokenizers without retraining. It then shows that a model can turn tokens into contextualized embeddings, where the same word gets a different vector depending on its context, using a small DeBERTa model that returns one vector per token.", "Text Embeddings (for Sentences and Whole Documents)": "Introduces models that return a single vector for a whole sentence or document, either by averaging token embeddings or, better, with a model trained for the job. The example uses the sentence-transformers library with all-mpnet-base-v2 to turn a sentence into a 768-value vector, and points forward to search and RAG as the main uses."}}, "HOLLM-6": {"chapter": "Prompt Engineering", "about": "A hands-on tour of prompting generative models: choosing and loading a model, the sampling settings that control randomness, the parts of a good prompt, and reasoning techniques. It ends with ways to control and validate output so an application does not break.", "ideas": ["Temperature and top_p set how random the output is, and a low setting suits tasks where you want the same answer each time.", "A prompt is made of parts such as persona, instruction, context, output format, audience, tone and data. Add or remove one at a time and watch the effect.", "Specificity is the most important prompting skill. Putting the instruction at the start or end helps because the middle of a long prompt is often forgotten.", "Showing examples (few-shot) and asking for step-by-step reasoning both improve results on harder tasks.", "Splitting one big task into several chained prompts gives each call its own focus and its own settings.", "For production, validate output with examples, constrained sampling (grammar) or fine-tuning, because free-form text can break your code."], "read_when": "Read it when you are writing or tuning prompts for a real feature and want a checklist of techniques with small code examples.", "sections": {"Controlling Model Output": "Explains that at each step the model scores every possible next token, and temperature and top_p decide how adventurous the choice is. Setting temperature to 0 or turning sampling off gives the most likely token every time, while higher values give varied answers. A table matches settings to uses, such as low values for email generation and high values for brainstorming.", "The Basic Ingredients of a Prompt": "Shows that a prompt can be just text to complete, but a useful one adds an instruction and the data it applies to. An output indicator such as ending with \"Sentiment:\" nudges the model to answer in one word. It then lists specificity, a way to reduce hallucination (allow \"I don't know\") and instruction position as shared techniques, and builds up a larger prompt from persona, context, format, audience and tone.", "In-Context Learning: Providing Examples": "Argues that showing the model examples works better than only describing the task. It defines zero-shot, one-shot and few-shot prompts and demonstrates a one-shot prompt with user and assistant turns that makes the model use an invented word correctly. It warns that examples are one piece of the puzzle and the model can still ignore them.", "Chain Prompting: Breaking up the Problem": "Shows how to split a task across several prompts, feeding the output of one into the next, for example name, then slogan, then sales pitch. Each call can get its own settings, such as a short token limit for the name and a longer one for the pitch. It lists uses such as validating a previous answer, running prompts in parallel and merging them, and writing stories in stages.", "Chain-of-Thought: Think Before Answering": "Shows that asking the model to write out its reasoning before the answer helps on multi-step problems like arithmetic, because each extra token gives it more to build on. It demonstrates both few-shot chain-of-thought with a worked example and the zero-shot version that just adds \"Let's think step-by-step\". The chapter also covers self-consistency (vote over several samples, n times slower) and tree-of-thought."}}, "HOLLM-7": {"chapter": "Advanced Text Generation Techniques and Tools", "about": "Uses LangChain to extend a plain LLM with reusable prompt templates, chained steps, conversation memory and agents. It stays practical, with small local models for chains and memory and GPT-3.5 for the agent example.", "ideas": ["A chain connects a prompt template to a model, so you define the template once and only pass variables.", "Complex tasks can be split into a sequence of smaller prompts, where each output feeds the next and you can read each intermediate result.", "LLMs are stateless, so \"memory\" means putting past conversation back into the prompt.", "Memory types trade off speed, token use and accuracy: a full buffer is simple but grows, a window forgets older turns, a summary is compact but costs an extra model call.", "An agent lets the model choose tools and the order of steps, using a loop of thought, action and observation (ReAct).", "Agents run without a human checking each step, so the chapter says you must design for reliability, for example by returning sources."], "read_when": "Read it when you are adding chat memory, chaining prompts or building a tool-using agent and want to see the moving parts in LangChain.", "sections": {"Agents: Creating a System of LLMs": "Defines an agent as a system where the model decides which actions to take and in what order, using tools plus a planning step. It explains ReAct, a loop of thought, action and observation, and builds a LangChain agent with web search and a calculator to find a laptop price and convert it to euros. It notes that smaller local models were not strong enough for this and that the lack of a human in the loop calls for careful design.", "Model I/O: Loading Quantized Models with LangChain": "Shows how to load a quantized GGUF version of Phi-3 through llama-cpp-python and LangChain, and explains quantization as storing weights with fewer bits to save memory and run faster, with a small loss of precision. It gives a rule of thumb to prefer models of at least 4 bits, and shows that the model returned nothing until the correct prompt template was applied.", "Chains: Extending the Capabilities of LLMs": "Introduces a chain as a link between an LLM and something extra, starting with a prompt template for Phi-3 so you do not repeat its special tokens. It then builds a sequential chain that writes a story title, then a character, then a summary, with each step feeding the next, and notes you get every intermediate piece as a separate output.", "Memory: Helping LLMs to Remember Conversations": "Demonstrates that a bare LLM forgets your name from one call to the next because it is stateless. The fix is to pass earlier conversation back in the prompt. The section introduces three options (buffer, windowed buffer, summary) and ends with a table of their pros and cons.", "Conversation Buffer": "Shows the simplest memory: paste the whole chat history into each prompt, using ConversationBufferMemory in LangChain, after which the model can recall the user's name. It is easy and loses nothing, but the prompt keeps growing and slows generation.", "Windowed Conversation Buffer": "Limits the history to the last k exchanges using ConversationBufferWindowMemory so the prompt does not outgrow the token limit. The example shows the model recalling a name but forgetting an age said in the first turn, which is the cost of dropping older turns.", "Conversation Summary": "Has a second LLM call summarise the conversation so far and puts that summary in the prompt, using ConversationSummaryMemory. This keeps the history small, but a detail like the exact first question may be lost and each turn needs an extra call, so the authors call it a trade-off between speed, memory and accuracy."}}, "HOLLM-8": {"chapter": "Semantic Search and Retrieval-Augmented Generation", "about": "Covers the three ways language models improve search: dense retrieval with embeddings, reranking, and RAG, where an LLM answers from retrieved text. It uses a small Interstellar example with Cohere's API and local models, and finishes with how to evaluate search and RAG.", "ideas": ["Dense retrieval embeds the query and the documents and returns the nearest vectors, so it finds matches by meaning when no keywords overlap.", "Dense retrieval always returns something, even when the corpus has no answer, so consider a distance threshold. It also struggles with exact phrases, which is why the book advises hybrid search with keyword matching.", "Long texts must be chunked because models have limited context. Chunk size, overlap and adding the title all change what the index can find.", "A reranker is a second stage that reads the query and each candidate together and reorders a shortlist from a first-stage search.", "RAG adds an LLM at the end of a search pipeline and prompts it with the question and retrieved text, which grounds the answer and reduces hallucination.", "Evaluate search with a test set of queries and relevance judgments, and RAG on fluency, citation quality, faithfulness and answer relevance."], "read_when": "Read it when you are building search or a RAG feature and need to choose between retrieval, reranking, chunking and evaluation approaches.", "sections": {"From Search to RAG": "Turns the earlier search code into RAG by adding an LLM at the end: the question and the top retrieved documents go to the model, which answers from that context, called grounded generation. One example uses Cohere's chat endpoint, which returns citations pointing at the source documents. A second example runs locally with Phi-3, a small embedding model, FAISS and a prompt template with a context slot, and notes that the local version loses span citations.", "Advanced RAG Techniques": "Lists upgrades to a basic RAG pipeline. Query rewriting turns a rambling chat message into a clean search query. Multi-query RAG runs several searches for a comparison question. Multi-hop RAG chains searches where the second depends on the first. Query routing picks which data source to search, and agentic RAG hands more of these decisions to the LLM, which only the largest models handled well at the time.", "RAG Evaluation": "Says RAG evaluation is still developing and describes human scoring on fluency, perceived utility, citation recall and citation precision. It then describes using a strong LLM as the judge to automate this, and names the Ragas library, which also scores faithfulness (is the answer consistent with the context) and answer relevance.", "Dense Retrieval": "Explains search as nearest-neighbour lookup: embed the documents once, embed each query, and return the closest vectors. The Cohere and FAISS example finds a good answer to a query that shares no keywords with it, which a BM25 comparison using rank_bm25 fails to do. It also lists caveats: results come back even if nothing is relevant, exact phrase lookup is better served by keywords, and models trained on one domain do poorly in another. The rest of the section covers chunking, nearest-neighbour libraries versus vector databases, and fine-tuning embeddings on query and result pairs.", "Reranking": "Shows a reranker as a final step in an existing search pipeline that takes the query and a shortlist and reorders it by relevance. A BM25 shortlist reranked with Cohere's Rerank endpoint promotes a better result than keyword scores alone, and the book reports a jump on the MIRACL benchmark from 36.5 to 62.8 nDCG@10. It explains that many rerankers are cross-encoders that read the query and a document together and output a score from 0 to 1.", "Retrieval Evaluation Metrics": "Explains that evaluating search needs a text archive, a set of queries and judgments of which documents are relevant to each. It walks through precision at a position, then average precision for one query, then mean average precision across all queries, so a system is rewarded for putting relevant results high. It names nDCG as the metric to use when relevance has degrees rather than yes or no.", "Chunking Long Texts": "Explains that models cannot embed very long texts, so you either keep one vector per document or, better, several vectors per document. One vector per document means embedding only the title or start, or averaging chunk vectors, and both lose information. Chunking by sentence is often too small, chunking by paragraph works for short paragraphs, and adding the title or overlapping neighbouring text keeps context. The best choice depends on your texts and queries."}}, "HOLLM-11": {"chapter": "Fine-Tuning Representation Models for Classification", "about": "Shows how to fine-tune BERT-style models, which output representations rather than text, for classification and related tasks. Topics include full fine-tuning with a classification head, the few-shot method SetFit, continued pretraining, and named-entity recognition.", "ideas": ["With enough labelled data, fine-tuning a representation model tends to beat using a pretrained one unchanged.", "In the movie review example, fine-tuning BERT reaches an F1 of 0.85 against 0.80 for the pretrained task-specific model from the earlier chapter, in a few minutes of training.", "Training the whole network, not just the classification head, is what the freezing-layers comparison is meant to show matters.", "SetFit reaches competitive results from a few labelled examples per class by building similar and dissimilar sentence pairs, fine-tuning an embedding model on them, then training a classifier on the embeddings.", "A pretrained model can be trained further on your own text with masked language modeling before the task step.", "Named-entity recognition classifies individual tokens, for example person, organisation and location, rather than whole documents."], "read_when": "Read it when a prompt-only classifier is not accurate enough and you have labelled data, or only a handful of labels, and want a trained classifier instead.", "sections": {}}, "HOLLM-12": {"chapter": "Fine-Tuning Generation Models", "about": "Walks through adapting a pretrained text generation model: the three training stages, supervised fine-tuning, parameter-efficient methods such as LoRA and QLoRA, and a full instruction-tuning run on TinyLlama. It then covers how to evaluate generative models and how preference tuning aligns them.", "ideas": ["High-quality LLMs come from three steps: pretraining on raw text, supervised fine-tuning to follow instructions, and preference tuning to match human preferences.", "A base model tends to continue a question instead of answering it, and fine-tuning on instruction and answer pairs fixes that.", "Full fine-tuning updates every weight and is costly, so parameter-efficient methods train only a small part of the model.", "LoRA trains two small matrices in place of each large weight matrix, and QLoRA also stores the frozen weights in 4 bits to cut memory.", "No single evaluation method is enough. Word-level metrics, public benchmarks, LLM-as-a-judge and human evaluation each show part of the picture, and the authors advise testing on your own use case.", "Preference tuning trains a reward model on accepted and rejected answers (PPO), or skips it with direct preference optimization (DPO)."], "read_when": "Read it when prompting and RAG are not enough and you are weighing whether to fine-tune a model yourself, or when you need to know how model quality is measured.", "sections": {"Evaluating Generative Models": "Argues that evaluating generative models is hard because one model is used for many tasks and its outputs vary, so no single metric works. It reviews word-level metrics such as perplexity, public benchmarks such as MMLU and HumanEval, leaderboards, LLM-as-a-judge and human evaluation such as Chatbot Arena, and notes the risk of overfitting to benchmarks. It advises evaluating on your intended use case and quotes Goodhart's Law, that a measure turned into a target stops being a good measure.", "The Three LLM Training Steps": "Lays out the pipeline: pretraining by next-token prediction on huge unlabelled text gives a base model that is hard for end users to work with. Supervised fine-tuning on instruction and answer data makes it follow instructions, and preference tuning then aligns its output with human preferences or safety expectations.", "Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT)": "Explains that updating all parameters is expensive in compute, time and storage, so PEFT trains a small part instead. It describes adapters, small modules added inside each Transformer block, citing a result where tuning about 3.6 percent of BERT's parameters came close to full fine-tuning. Specialised adapters can be swapped on the same base model.", "Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA)": "Shows how LoRA replaces training a large weight matrix with training two much smaller matrices whose product approximates the change, for example 20 weights instead of 100 in a toy case. It argues this works because models have low intrinsic dimension, and gives a GPT-3 sized estimate of about 197 thousand parameters per block at rank 8. It then explains QLoRA, which adds blockwise 4-bit quantization of the frozen weights to cut memory further.", "Instruction Tuning with QLoRA": "A code walkthrough that instruction-tunes TinyLlama with QLoRA on 3,000 UltraChat conversations formatted in a chat template. It loads the model in 4 bits with bitsandbytes, sets LoRA options with peft (rank, alpha, target modules), trains with trl's SFTTrainer, then merges the adapter into the base model. It reports the model loading in about 1 GB of VRAM instead of about 4 GB, and the tuned model now follows instructions."}}, "PE-2": {"chapter": "Understanding LLMs", "about": "Explains how an LLM works from the outside in: a mimic of text, tokens, one-token-at-a-time generation, sampling with temperature, and a light look at the transformer. It keeps to what a prompt writer needs and skips the maths.", "ideas": ["An LLM is a document completion engine. Text goes in, text comes out, and the model imitates the kinds of documents it was trained on.", "Every model has its own fixed tokenizer. Length, speed, price and the context window are all measured in tokens, so count them with that tokenizer.", "The model writes one token at a time and cannot pause, backtrack or take a token back. Any checking or retrying has to be built by you.", "Temperature 0 is for correctness and near-repeatable output. Higher values give variety, and values above 1 get worse the longer the text runs.", "Logprobs show the probability the model gave each token, which you can use as a rough confidence signal.", "The model reads the prompt once, from start to end, so the order of the prompt matters."], "read_when": "Read it when you want a mental model for odd behaviour such as repetition, running out of context, or different answers on every run.", "sections": {"Counting Tokens": "Each model has a fixed tokenizer, so you count tokens with the matching one. The count sets reading time, generation time, cost and the context window, which must hold the prompt and the completion together. English averages about four characters per token, while other languages, digit strings and keys cost more tokens per character.", "One Token at a Time": "The model predicts one token, adds it to the prompt and predicts again, which is called autoregressive generation. It cannot stop to think or erase a token, and it can lock into a repeating pattern it started by chance. The advice is to detect and filter repetitive output or add some randomness.", "Temperature and Probabilities": "The model scores every possible token and then a sampling step picks one. Temperature 0 takes the likeliest token and suits cases where correctness matters, though results are still not perfectly deterministic. Around 1 the output follows the training data's spread, and above 1 it turns erratic. Higher temperatures make sense when you generate several candidates and filter them."}}, "PE-3": {"chapter": "Moving to Chat", "about": "Shows how base models became chat and tool-calling assistants through human-feedback tuning and the ChatML format, and how the API changed to match. Its point is that a chat model is still completing a document, now a transcript.", "ideas": ["A base model only completes documents and can imitate anything on the internet, so fine-tuning turns it into a helpful, honest and safe assistant.", "Models are trained to follow the system message closely, so it is the place for rules and persona.", "Role markers are reserved tokens that users cannot type through the chat API, which blocks forged system or assistant messages. This only holds if you keep user and retrieved text out of the system message.", "Chat tuning has costs: chattier answers, a possible drop in other skills (the alignment tax) and less variety. A plain completion API is still better when you want the raw answer with nothing to parse out.", "Two conversations exist: user with assistant, and your app with the model. The app can add context the user never sees, which the book compares to writing a play.", "Tool calling is the same trick again: the transcript just gains a special syntax for requesting a function and getting its result."], "read_when": "Read it when you build on a chat API and want to know what the roles really do, why the system message matters for safety, or whether a completion model would fit better.", "sections": {"Chat Completion API": "Shows a basic call with a system message and a user message, and the JSON reply, which includes finish reason and token usage. Warns against putting the user's request or retrieved file text into the system message, because that gives up the protection the special tokens provide. Lists useful parameters: max_tokens, stop, stream, logprobs, n and temperature, with temperature the most important for prompt work."}}, "PE-4": {"chapter": "Designing LLM Applications", "about": "Frames an LLM application as a loop that turns the user's problem into a document for the model and turns the completion back into something useful. It then shows how the loop grows with state, outside content, reasoning and tools, and ends with evaluation.", "ideas": ["The app is a transformation layer between the user's domain and the model's text domain. The loop may run once or many times.", "A good prompt meets four needs: it resembles training documents (the Little Red Riding Hood principle), holds all relevant information, steers the model to an answer, and has a natural stopping point.", "The feedforward pass has four steps: retrieve context, cut it into snippets, score and prioritise the snippets, and assemble them within the token budget.", "Complexity grows along four lines: stored state, external content (RAG), deeper reasoning and tool use.", "Models make mistakes, so tools that change the real world need care, such as asking the user before a booking.", "Evaluate before launch and keep measuring after launch."], "read_when": "Read it first, before the technique chapters, to get the overall shape of an LLM app and a place to hang each later technique.", "sections": {"The Anatomy of the Loop": "Describes the app as a loop between the user domain and the model domain and walks once around it. The prompt must look like familiar documents, include all relevant facts, lead to an answer and make the model stop; a travel recommendation written as a homework sheet shows all four. The completion then goes back to the user as text, parsed data or a function call, and the choice of model size trades quality against cost and latency.", "Exploring the Complexity of the Loop": "Starts from a stateless single-call app and lists four ways it grows. Chat needs stored history, which you truncate or summarise when it gets long. Outside knowledge comes from retrieval. Reasoning improves if the model thinks out loud first, since it has no inner monologue. Tools let it search or act, and write actions need extra caution."}}, "PE-5": {"chapter": "Prompt Content", "about": "Covers where prompt content comes from, split into static content that clarifies the task and dynamic content gathered for each request. Most pages go to few-shot examples, finding context, retrieval (RAG) and summarisation.", "ideas": ["Static content is hardcoded text that defines the task. Dynamic content is fetched per request and describes this particular case.", "Clarifying the question makes the model treat every input the same way. Ask for what you want, give a reason, and avoid absolutes.", "Few-shot examples teach format and style well, but they scale badly with long context, anchor the model on your examples and can plant accidental patterns.", "Gather more context than you need and score each item, thinking about latency, what can be prepared in advance, and whether items can be compared.", "Irrelevant retrieved text gets over-interpreted by the model (the Chekhov's gun fallacy), so retrieval quality matters.", "Summarise with the final task in mind, and use hierarchical summaries for text that exceeds the context window."], "read_when": "Read it when you decide what goes into a prompt: how to write instructions and examples, what context to fetch and how to retrieve it.", "sections": {"Static Content": "Static content is the fixed text that states and clarifies the task. The book says clarifying the question is harder and more important than people expect, because it makes the model handle all inputs the same way. Its rules of thumb are to ask for positives instead of negatives, to add a reason to each command, and to avoid absolutes; in chat models the system message is the place for these instructions.", "Few-Shot Prompting": "Examples show the model the format, style and unstated rules, which is often easier than writing them out, and they work especially well for output format. Three drawbacks: they scale poorly when each case carries a lot of context, they bias the model toward the spread of your examples, and they can suggest false patterns such as order. The advice is to cover every main class and edge case, shuffle the examples, test the choice, and skip them if the task is already clear.", "Dynamic Content": "Dynamic content is the per-request context, and gathering it is where most design time goes. Three things shape it: latency, whether you can prepare it ahead, and whether items can be compared with a score. To find sources, mind-map what the model would like to know, list what the app can actually get, and sort that list by closeness to the app and by how stable it is.", "Retrieval-Augmented Generation": "RAG means fetching relevant snippets and putting them in the prompt. Irrelevant snippets can mislead the model, so retrieval is treated as a search problem. It compares word-matching search (Jaccard, TF-IDF, BM25), which is simple and easy to debug, with embedding search, which matches meaning but is opaque. It also covers cutting documents into overlapping chunks, choosing an embedding model and a vector store, and a small worked example."}}, "PE-6": {"chapter": "Assembling the Prompt", "about": "Shows how to arrange the gathered pieces into one prompt: the shape of a good prompt, which kind of document to imitate and how to format snippets. It ends by treating assembly as choosing which pieces fit in the token budget.", "ideas": ["A good prompt runs from an introduction through context to a refocus that repeats the question (the sandwich) and a transition that begins the answer.", "The end of the prompt has the most influence and the start is recalled well. The early middle is used least, which the book calls the Valley of Meh.", "Pick a document type for the prompt: an advice conversation, an analytic report or a structured document.", "Elastic snippets keep several lengths of the same content, so the assembler asks which is the largest version that fits.", "Prompt elements relate by position, importance (scores or priority tiers) and dependency (requirements and incompatibilities).", "Start with a minimal prompt builder that keeps the end of your content, then move to a greedy add-or-remove method as the app grows."], "read_when": "Read it when you write the code that turns instructions and retrieved context into the final prompt, especially under a token limit.", "sections": {"Anatomy of the Ideal Prompt": "Lays out the parts of a prompt: an introduction that sets up the task, a run of context elements, a refocus that restates the main question, and a transition that starts the answer. Models weight the end most and the early middle least (the Valley of Meh), so key items belong outside it. It then compares document types: advice conversation, analytic report and structured document.", "Elastic Snippets": "One piece of content can be included at many sizes, from the bare passage to the passage with lots of surrounding text. Instead of asking whether a snippet fits, the assembler asks for the biggest version that fits. The alternative is to create separate overlapping elements and mark them as incompatible so only one is used.", "Putting It All Together": "Treats prompt assembly as an optimisation problem: choose the elements with the most value while respecting requirements, incompatibilities and a token budget (the context window minus room for the reply). No standard tool solves it, so you write your own. Begin with a minimal builder that takes the end of the content, then use a greedy method that adds the best element each step or removes the weakest."}}, "PE-7": {"chapter": "Taming the Model", "about": "Looks at the completion side: what a good response looks like, how to find and cut it, and how to read the model's confidence from logprobs. The second half is a practical guide to choosing and fine-tuning a model.", "ideas": ["A completion has a preamble, the main answer and a postscript. Preambles cost tokens but are worth keeping when they hold chain-of-thought reasoning.", "Make the start and end of the answer easy to recognise, and use stop sequences so you do not pay for tokens you will discard.", "For classification, give each option a different first token, because options that share a first token add up their probabilities and can win unfairly.", "You can calibrate a classifier by shifting logprobs by a constant, and many APIs offer logit bias for this.", "Choose a model by weighing quality, latency, cost, hosting effort and features, and do not hardcode the choice.", "Fine-tuning comes in kinds: full, LoRA and soft prompting. LoRA mostly teaches format, style and priors rather than new skills."], "read_when": "Read it when you need to parse model output reliably, stop rambling, use the model as a classifier, or choose between hosted, open source and fine-tuned models.", "sections": {"Choosing the Model": "Recommends not baking the model choice into your code, because the market moves fast and a unifying API such as LiteLLM helps. It weighs answer quality, latency, cost, hosting effort, capabilities and non-negotiable preferences such as data location. It says to pick the smallest model that does the job, prototype with a slightly larger one, and self-host open models only at scale. It ends with fine-tuning: full, LoRA and soft prompting, and a revised Little Red Riding Hood rule that your prompt should look like the fine-tuning documents and not like the original training ones."}}, "PE-8": {"chapter": "Conversational Agency", "about": "Shows how to give a chat model abilities beyond talking: calling tools, reasoning before answering, and keeping the right context in a long conversation. It ends by building a small working agent and discussing the user interface for it.", "ideas": ["A bare model has no private or current data, is weak at maths and cannot act. Tools fix all three.", "The application owns the tool loop: it parses the call, runs the real function and appends the result to the messages.", "Tool definitions should be few, simple and well named, with few arguments. Remove arguments the app already knows, and return useful error messages.", "For risky tools, let the model request the action but have the application ask the user before running it. Asking the model to check first is not reliable.", "Having the model reason before it answers (chain of thought, ReAct) usually gives better results, because it has no inner monologue.", "An agent's context has a preamble, prior conversation, attached artifacts and the current exchange. Deciding what to include is a trade-off between confusion and missing information."], "read_when": "Read it when you add tools to an assistant, build the loop that lets the model call functions, or need design rules for tool definitions.", "sections": {"Chain of Thought": "The model has no inner monologue, so an answer given first is a guess and the explanation after it is a rationalisation. Making it reason before answering, either with few-shot examples or with a cue such as asking for step-by-step thinking, raised accuracy in the cited studies, for example on math word problems from about 20 percent to 60 percent. A related idea trained models to use blank pause tokens to buy thinking time.", "Tool Usage": "Explains why a model needs tools: it lacks private and current information, is poor at arithmetic and cannot change anything in the world. The fix is to tell it which tools exist and when to use them, while the application parses the call, runs the real API and puts the result into the next prompt. A small thermostat example shows tool definitions as JSON schema and a function that handles messages and tool calls.", "LLMs Trained for Tool Usage": "Shows that tool calling is a fine-tuned model plus API convenience and is still document completion. Tool definitions are placed in the system message as TypeScript-like text, so they count against the token budget. A call is a chain of small decisions: who speaks, whether to call a tool, which tool, which arguments and values, and when to stop.", "Guidelines for Tool Definitions": "Give the model few tools that split the domain cleanly, and do not paste your whole web API into the prompt. Use clear names, short definitions and few simple arguments. Watch for made-up argument values such as placeholder names, and remove or default arguments the app already knows. Keep tool outputs lean, make error messages explain what to fix, and for dangerous tools intercept the call in the application and get the user's approval.", "ReAct: Iterative Reasoning and Action": "ReAct is a loop of thought, action and observation: the model writes a thought, calls a tool such as search, lookup or finish, reads the result and repeats until it finishes. In the original study it first did worse than plain prompting because the examples did not teach tool use well, then led after light fine-tuning. Thinking before acting matters most in decision tasks where the agent must track a goal.", "Building a Conversational Agent": "Observes that the thermostat example is nearly an agent already. Two things are missing: a way for the user to type to it, and a loop around the message-processing function.", "Managing Conversations": "Presents the loop that holds the full message list, reads user input, runs the model and any tool calls until the assistant replies, then waits for the next input. Because it keeps the prior conversation, the agent can undo its own earlier change, as the sample dialogue shows. Swapping the system message and tools changes its behaviour, but real use also needs an API, error handling and logging."}}, "PE-9": {"chapter": "LLM Workflows", "about": "Argues that a complex job is better done by a fixed workflow of small tasks than by one free-roaming agent, and shows how to design one. It then covers more advanced designs where the model itself directs the flow.", "ideas": ["There is a trade-off between generality and strength. A narrow, structured workflow can finish complex tasks that a general chat agent cannot.", "A conversational agent fails on a large multistep job: it cannot do the work, produces generic output, gets distracted by a long prompt and is hard to debug.", "Define each task with a clear input and output schema and test it alone. Modular tasks make failures easy to locate.", "Do not use an LLM where ordinary code works. Use a cheap model for easy tasks and a human check for actions that cannot be undone.", "Prefer a pipeline or a DAG. Add cycles only when needed, with a retry limit, and keep any loops inside a task.", "Use a basic workflow first. Let the LLM steer the flow only when the problem needs that freedom."], "read_when": "Read it when a job has several steps such as extract, decide, write and send, and you are choosing between one agent and a fixed pipeline, or designing the tasks and their data shapes.", "sections": {"Would a Conversational Agent Suffice?": "Tries the Shopify plug-in marketing job with a conversational agent and shows it failing in stages. With no tools it only writes a plan, with search and email tools it sends generic form letters, and with a long system message it gets distracted. It also has no way to process work in units and is hard to fix when something fails. The conclusion is to split the job into tasks inside a workflow.", "Basic LLM Workflows": "Gives five steps: define the goal, specify tasks, implement tasks, implement the workflow, then optimise. Each task needs well-defined inputs and outputs, and can be a prompt template, a forced tool call that extracts structured data, or a task with chain of thought or a self-correction pass. Some tasks need no LLM at all, and some need a human or a different model size.", "Advanced LLM Workflows": "Lets the model direct the flow. Options are a workflow agent with tools that map to tasks, tasks that are themselves agents, tasks created on the fly, and a growing task list the agent keeps reprioritising. The book warns that more autonomy makes the system less stable and harder to reason about, so try a basic workflow first.", "Assembling the Workflow": "Describes tasks connected as a state machine. A pipeline is simplest, a DAG lets later tasks reuse earlier results, and a cyclic graph allows retry loops but adds complexity such as attempt counters and carrying data back. It advises hiding loops inside a task, and notes batch processing is simpler than streaming.", "Stateful Task Agents": "Gives each work item its own long-lived agent that changes the item and tells dependent agents when something changes, as with files of a web page. A workflow agent can orchestrate them, or you can build a dependency graph, taking care with circular dependencies. Users can also talk to the agent that owns an item.", "Example Workflow: Shopify Plug-in Marketing": "Builds the four tasks: collect storefront pages, extract details with specific questions, brainstorm and then write a plug-in concept in two steps, and write the email after planning a strategy. The output was decent. Suggested improvements are more variety in ideas, a feasibility check, a Reflexion feedback step, offline test examples for each task, and recording real traffic for A/B tests."}}, "PE-10": {"chapter": "Evaluating LLM Applications", "about": "Explains how to test an LLM app before launch and after, starting from the claim that evaluation should be the first code you write. It covers how to find examples, how to score free-text answers, and how to learn from real users.", "ideas": ["Build evaluation first. It tells you whether each change is a step forward, so everything else moves faster.", "Offline evaluation is safe, scales and exists before launch. Online evaluation tests on real users and is the final proof.", "Test the whole loop like a regression test, and also test critical single model passes like unit tests. Record latency and token use in every test.", "Start with an example suite of 5 to 20 inputs that you compare by eye, then grow it with mined records, app data or LLM-generated samples.", "Score by matching a gold standard, by functional checks, or by an LLM judge, from easiest to hardest. A judge should grade a third party and should be checked against human agreement.", "In an online A/B test, pick metrics tied to real value, such as acceptance or impact, and add guardrail metrics for failures."], "read_when": "Read it before you ship or change an LLM feature, when you need a plan for testing prompts, scoring free-text answers or comparing versions on real users.", "sections": {"What Are We Even Testing?": "Evaluation can target the model, a single prompt interaction, or the whole application. It recommends whole-loop tests like regression tests plus unit-style tests of the critical single passes, and which you lean on depends on whether you are swapping models, tuning prompts or changing the architecture. Latency and token counts should be recorded in all of them.", "Offline Evaluation": "Offline evaluation means running examples without live users, and the section advises starting simple. You need a source of example inputs and a way to judge outputs, and the section ends with questions to ask about each source and each kind of test.", "Example Suites": "The simplest harness is 5 to 20 representative inputs, a script that builds the prompts and gets completions, and a way to compare output between prompt versions, such as files in version control. It gives no automatic pass or fail, so you judge differences by eye. It can start before you have any quality criteria, and the book illustrates it with pull request summaries at GitHub.", "Evaluating Solutions": "Gives three scoring methods, easiest first. Matching a gold standard works for yes or no and classification answers, and partial matching checks one key aspect such as whether the right tool was called. Functional tests run or parse the output, for example running a code suite. The last is asking an LLM to judge, which gives relative rather than absolute quality, works best when it grades a third party, and should be checked against human raters.", "SOMA Assessment": "SOMA is the book's recipe for reliable LLM grading: Specific questions, Ordinal scales whose levels are described instead of yes or no, and Multiple Aspects such as intent and execution, or relevance, truth and completeness. It says to split vague questions like 'just right' into two, and to state the criteria before showing the item to grade. Trust it only after checking that adding the model to a pool of human raters does not increase disagreement.", "Online Evaluation": "Online evaluation tests with real users, usually through A/B tests with chosen metrics and guardrail metrics. It ranks five kinds of metric from direct feedback to incidental ones such as latency, with functional correctness, acceptance and impact in between. Thumbs buttons are sparse and biased, so look first for an acceptance or impact metric. Online tests have little bandwidth, so only test ideas that already survived offline."}}};
